WEBVTT

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:11.020
Hemant, thanks for having me to
your office here in New York

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.440
to talk about what's going on
in the market, your Q2 letter,

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:16.900
and how you see the state of
venture and how it's changing.

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:18.080
Thanks for doing this.

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:22.960
To start us off, we're in mid-July right
now. What's going on in the market?

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:23.920
It's wild out there.

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:27.320
I walked into this year, when
we had this conversation with

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:29.860
the team, where we said, "We
just have to fundamentally

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:33.440
recalibrate what scale means,
what we're really striving for."

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:38.620
And the data points are when a company
like NVIDIA can add a trillion dollars of

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:44.420
market cap in 100 days, or Anthropic,
which we now have a very large investment

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:48.860
in, is adding the kind of revenue it's
adding every month, billions of dollars.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:49.380
Yeah.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:50.880
Ten plus billion dollars, often.

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:53.540
You just sort of say, "Wait a minute.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:57.080
The scale that we're operating at
and the concentration of value

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:00.620
that's starting to happen in a handful
of companies is pretty stunning."

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:04.320
And so the corollary to that is,
what are the founders of this

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:08.280
generation supposed to be doing so
that their work can actually matter?

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:08.940
Hmm.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:11.980
And I'll tell you, when I first
got in the business in 2000,

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:17.040
our first institutional fund was 200
million bucks, and the goal was invest

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:20.360
in some company where you own 20%, sell
it for a billion, and return the fund.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:20.880
Yep.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:25.400
And unicorn, 10 years later, the
word unicorn was a measure of scale.

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:29.180
Last decade, it was all about
can you get to decacorns?

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:34.100
But today, when you have
multiple, one, two, three, four

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:37.660
trillion-dollar companies that
are going to be five, six, seven,

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:40.600
eight, nine, ten trillion-dollar
companies in a few years, building a

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:45.400
billion-dollar business or a $10 billion
business, I think it'd be financially interesting,

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:49.560
but not compelling for founders
to be their life's work, because

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:52.500
great founders want to make
a dent in the universe.

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:53.020
Hmm.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:57.720
And so just rethinking what is a scale
that our companies have to get to?

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:01.300
How do we get them there in
a world where there's so much

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:05.240
momentum towards these
large companies vertically

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:07.860
integrating into every
dimension, every profit pool?

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:11.460
That, to me, is what the focus of
the last six months for me has been.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:14.620
Yeah. And so it seems
from both, you had this

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:18.260
fairly controversial X article
that came out yesterday.

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:19.320
And then-

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:20.040
Was it controversial?

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:21.040
I think it was a little bit controversial.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:22.180
Okay. I didn't see.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:27.080
I think we can talk about it. I'll ask you
the thesis, and then we can go into it.

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:31.320
But to stop playing the markup game, which
I'd love for you to describe the thesis.

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:32.720
I think it's related to what you just said.

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:35.480
But what does it mean to stop playing
the markup game? What is the markup game?

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:38.440
I think there's a couple things
in that. So I'll tell you,

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:42.960
when the correction happened
after COVID, the very first

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:46.320
thing that I did, and Paul Fielding
was our CFO, he and I sort of

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:48.040
decided we're going to do something

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:50.600
draconian, is we marked
our portfolio down by 40%.

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:51.120
Yep.

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:54.280
And our LPs told us we were the
first firm to actually do that.

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:58.060
And the reason was, we didn't want
our investors thinking there's

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:01.840
value in these companies at the
valuation that we're getting done

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:06.840
in the exuberance of post-COVID, that
everything's going digital. That wasn't there.

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:10.920
And the second is, as investors, you
don't want your next-generation investors

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:14.960
coming and thinking, "Look how good an
investor I am because I've got this markup."

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:15.480
Yep.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:18.180
And so we took it down, became rational.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:21.220
We did it again earlier this
year when Claude happened-

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:21.740
Hmm...

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:22.780
and the whole terminal value

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:27.220
question was being asked of software companies,
which is a very reasonable question.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:30.860
So the reason I wrote that was
actually for the next-generation

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:35.640
investors to understand, just trying
to get marks in the heat of the

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:41.360
market isn't really the best way to
create value or be helpful to founders.

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:41.880
Yeah.

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:45.040
And if I go back to my comment
about scale, and the fact

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:47.260
that these companies need
to get to be a lot bigger,

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:50.080
you're not going to get there by
bludgeoning them with equity.

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:54.380
So the whole point there was,
rather than just thinking about...

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.320
Because every time we go, founders raise

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:58.240
capital, you're taking something away
from them. You're taking their stock.

00:03:58.260 --> 00:03:58.760
Yep.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:01.880
Right? And so how do we get them
to the promised land without

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:04.920
having to continue to do that when
things are way more capital intense?

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:06.720
If you look at infrastructure
stuff, you look

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:09.700
at resilience stuff, you
need a lot more capital.

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:12.880
The answer can't be just
keep marking up stock to

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:15.320
finance these, and we need an
innovation in the industry.

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:17.999
So my point was more get everybody in our

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:20.040
industry to think like
entrepreneurs ourselves.

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:23.120
I think we get complacent that
we sit on a pedestal, and we

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:25.700
get to play God and say yes, no
all day long and give you money.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:28.460
No, we need to be entrepreneurial
about how do we serve these

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:32.140
founders in a much more compelling
way, given the needs are

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:35.820
changing and given the scale they have
to get to is fundamentally changing.

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:38.760
Yeah. I think the controversy
might have come from,

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:42.120
one, I think General Catalyst
came up in an era where

00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:45.940
you could invest at a certain
price, mark it back up.

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:50.060
I think Peter Thiel is the one who
famously said that the most bullish

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:54.140
signal is when a firm marks its own position
up within a certain amount of months.

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:55.820
And so that is the environment.

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:59.940
And then now when you're in a position
where you have access to all of these

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:05.060
different types of financing structures
that you can provide to founders, it kind

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:10.140
of came across as the thing that matters
now is capital, and picking the right

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:14.340
companies and relationships, the thing that
used to matter, don't matter as much anymore.

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:16.020
Do you view it that way?

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:18.000
So actually, that's not
what it was meant to say.

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:20.960
So for us, when we look
at our venture business,

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:25.140
we look at ourselves as a seed firm,
just like you run a seed business.

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:25.660
Yep.

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:27.600
Okay? There's a reason why we have three

00:05:27.620 --> 00:05:29.640
senior partners that build
their own seed firms-

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:30.160
Yeah...

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:33.500
at the center of it, which is, hey,
we really want to be seed first.

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:37.260
So that relationship and building
that trust and ownership in the

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:41.400
beginning and navigating ambiguity is
what venture is really about, right?

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:44.560
The point, though, is from
there, the journey that these

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:47.060
founders now have and the
capital they need and the

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:50.300
sophistication of capital they need
isn't just about growth equity.

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:50.960
Yeah.

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:54.800
And we have innovated some. We
created a customer value fund. We

00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:56.480
just raised

00:05:57.540 --> 00:05:58.900
for a global resilience

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:02.500
public vehicle to be able
to take some companies out.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:04.880
So we're innovating, but
I'm telling you, I sort of

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:07.400
sit there and say, "We're not
innovating nearly enough."

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:09.920
And so the whole point was to
actually just say, "Hey, everybody,

00:06:09.940 --> 00:06:13.360
start thinking about innovating so
we have more capital innovation."

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:17.440
And this is not about financial engineering,
it's about financial innovation.

00:06:17.948 --> 00:06:18.928
What's the difference?

00:06:19.528 --> 00:06:20.628
Well, I think

00:06:21.988 --> 00:06:25.348
financial engineering is creating
community EBITDA with WeWork.

00:06:25.368 --> 00:06:26.508
That's financial engineering.

00:06:26.688 --> 00:06:32.008
Financial innovation is how do you come up
with structures that create this sort of

00:06:32.848 --> 00:06:35.848
absorption of AI in society,
just like the corporate

00:06:35.868 --> 00:06:38.228
bond market we created to
finance the railroads, right?

00:06:38.248 --> 00:06:42.068
These are these sort of
large national-scale,

00:06:42.088 --> 00:06:44.828
global-scale transformations
that you go through.

00:06:45.188 --> 00:06:49.248
Innovation in finance is as much a part
of it as innovation in technology.

00:06:49.448 --> 00:06:49.948
I see.

00:06:49.467 --> 00:06:51.148
Who's going to do that if
it's not our industry?

00:06:51.448 --> 00:06:53.088
We are the ones that are
closest to what the needs of

00:06:53.108 --> 00:06:55.008
these founders are, and I just
don't see enough of that,

00:06:55.028 --> 00:06:57.728
and I think it's because we're
complacent with the markup game.

00:06:57.748 --> 00:06:58.248
Mm-hmm.

00:06:57.768 --> 00:06:59.788
Because we think that's good
enough, and I think we're not

00:06:59.808 --> 00:07:03.408
serving these founders right if
we're not pushing ourselves harder.

00:07:03.468 --> 00:07:05.868
It is a question that I've even
written about before, which

00:07:05.888 --> 00:07:10.348
is, does Blackstone become GC
before GC becomes Blackstone?

00:07:10.388 --> 00:07:13.148
How do you think about
who has the advantages

00:07:13.168 --> 00:07:15.188
there in working with these
companies and provide it?

00:07:15.328 --> 00:07:17.628
Is it the person who has the
financing experience with

00:07:17.648 --> 00:07:20.648
all these structures that
can learn tech or vice versa?

00:07:21.608 --> 00:07:22.427
Look, I think

00:07:23.188 --> 00:07:26.068
there was a version of this that
happened before as well because

00:07:26.208 --> 00:07:29.068
most venture firms, including us,
we were early-stage venture firms.

00:07:29.208 --> 00:07:31.768
And you had these public
hedge funds that came down

00:07:31.788 --> 00:07:34.068
starting crossover investing,
and there was this whole

00:07:34.088 --> 00:07:37.588
idea of is the market sort of
reinstitutionalizing into platforms?

00:07:37.608 --> 00:07:39.468
But who's going to
capture most of the value?

00:07:39.648 --> 00:07:43.568
Folks like us that fundamentally
understand technology and where it's

00:07:43.588 --> 00:07:47.928
leading in the world and have to go
get access to more scale of capital-

00:07:47.948 --> 00:07:48.448
Yeah...

00:07:48.228 --> 00:07:51.748
or people that are trying
to commoditize providing

00:07:51.768 --> 00:07:54.428
capital to founders because
they have scaled capital.

00:07:54.728 --> 00:07:56.008
And I think if you look at

00:07:57.088 --> 00:07:58.928
the dynamic around that in
the last 10 years, I would

00:07:58.948 --> 00:08:02.008
actually argue that there's
a handful of firms that have

00:08:02.028 --> 00:08:06.028
really innovated and are starting
to do things at the larger scale.

00:08:06.268 --> 00:08:11.648
And the reality is, frankly, if I think
about alpha that needs to be created

00:08:11.668 --> 00:08:14.988
in this world of AI with existing
businesses, that's where this really applies.

00:08:15.008 --> 00:08:15.508
Yeah.

00:08:16.268 --> 00:08:21.348
So much more of the alpha is about
taking AI, operationally innovating, and

00:08:21.368 --> 00:08:24.568
then driving abundance from a product
perspective to serve your customers.

00:08:24.928 --> 00:08:27.888
Way more of it comes from that than can you

00:08:27.908 --> 00:08:30.828
actually cost cut and financial engineering.

00:08:30.848 --> 00:08:37.668
So I would say the advantages to firms like
ours that kind of grew up in the era of

00:08:38.088 --> 00:08:43.048
truly driving, building the world's most
important companies, not optimizing profit.

00:08:43.068 --> 00:08:43.568
So I

00:08:44.228 --> 00:08:47.648
actually would tell you, I've had
conversations with many of the large PE firms.

00:08:47.668 --> 00:08:50.388
They themselves don't see future
in traditional PE anymore.

00:08:50.408 --> 00:08:53.268
Hmm. So how do you think
about-- You have these

00:08:53.288 --> 00:08:55.548
examples, right, that
you call the living labs.

00:08:56.028 --> 00:08:57.148
How do you think about...

00:08:57.688 --> 00:08:59.528
Maybe take me inside one or two of them.

00:08:59.968 --> 00:09:02.568
What do you do differently
than a big PE firm coming down

00:09:02.588 --> 00:09:05.688
and saying, "We're going to
buy companies and apply AI"?

00:09:05.948 --> 00:09:06.948
Because you've grown up in tech-

00:09:06.968 --> 00:09:07.468
Yeah...

00:09:06.968 --> 00:09:07.648
what do you do differently?

00:09:07.668 --> 00:09:11.308
Yeah. So look, when a great
founder starts a company,

00:09:11.328 --> 00:09:13.588
their ambition generally is
to go change some industry.

00:09:13.608 --> 00:09:14.108
Yep.

00:09:13.908 --> 00:09:17.848
Okay? And our view is that when you want to

00:09:17.868 --> 00:09:20.988
transform an industry, no
single company can do it.

00:09:21.108 --> 00:09:21.608
Hmm.

00:09:21.548 --> 00:09:24.528
Okay? And then I said this
when we were deep in the

00:09:24.548 --> 00:09:27.188
healthcare work in the COVID
era, and I was like, "The

00:09:27.208 --> 00:09:30.288
Amazon of healthcare is not going
to be a trillion-dollar company.

00:09:30.308 --> 00:09:31.688
It's going to be a
trillion-dollar ecosystem."

00:09:31.708 --> 00:09:32.208
Hmm.

00:09:32.248 --> 00:09:35.708
Okay? So our theory, for example, if I go to

00:09:35.728 --> 00:09:38.448
healthcare for a second, let's
take the Summa as a living lab,

00:09:39.168 --> 00:09:41.048
which is the Akron-based
system that we bought.

00:09:41.168 --> 00:09:45.328
Our belief is that if we want a
proactive, affordable, accessible

00:09:45.348 --> 00:09:50.228
healthcare system, we have to turn
these local institutions, these

00:09:50.248 --> 00:09:54.548
hospitals, which are poorly run,
they're all teetering on bankruptcy-

00:09:54.568 --> 00:09:55.068
Yep...

00:09:55.268 --> 00:09:58.808
into AI native hospitals that
are profitable, that can

00:09:58.828 --> 00:10:02.248
invest in their communities, and
can drive that proactive care.

00:10:02.408 --> 00:10:05.688
Okay? Now, when I think about, okay,
we have to go do that, let's go

00:10:05.708 --> 00:10:08.008
buy a hospital, and I sit down and say,
"What are we going to do with it?"

00:10:09.088 --> 00:10:11.468
There's no company that can go
in there and go accomplish that.

00:10:11.588 --> 00:10:14.348
So what's happening inside of
Summa right now is we have our

00:10:14.368 --> 00:10:20.068
own AI team, Percepta, and we
have, I think, eight of our AI

00:10:20.508 --> 00:10:23.588
healthcare founders that are targeting
different parts of the stack.

00:10:23.908 --> 00:10:25.028
They're all working together.

00:10:25.988 --> 00:10:29.428
And when you look at the corporate
metrics that transform a hospital

00:10:29.448 --> 00:10:32.668
like that into the future version of
what I think it needs to look like,

00:10:33.488 --> 00:10:36.808
they're work being done by different
companies that toggle each metric.

00:10:36.968 --> 00:10:39.388
And so you all of a sudden get
this operational view of this

00:10:39.408 --> 00:10:42.468
AI native hospital where all
these companies are contributing,

00:10:43.028 --> 00:10:46.588
and they learn a lot from doing
that at enterprise-wide scale.

00:10:46.608 --> 00:10:47.108
Hmm.

00:10:46.868 --> 00:10:48.688
Because there's not the inertia
of, oh, we're going to do

00:10:48.708 --> 00:10:53.168
pilots and do incremental stuff
and years of BAN certifications.

00:10:53.188 --> 00:10:56.108
No, we're going in there, and
as long as these solutions are

00:10:56.128 --> 00:10:59.508
proven, because we don't want to be
irresponsible with people's lives-

00:10:59.608 --> 00:11:00.108
Right...

00:10:59.648 --> 00:11:02.628
which is the only kinds of companies we
bring in here, we're going all the way.

00:11:03.228 --> 00:11:05.488
But we're going to go all the
way as an ecosystem of companies.

00:11:05.508 --> 00:11:07.688
So I think this collaboration
between founders to say

00:11:07.708 --> 00:11:10.428
we're going to together
transform an industry, these

00:11:10.448 --> 00:11:13.668
living labs are necessary environments
to be able to go do that in.

00:11:13.948 --> 00:11:16.228
Janice is another example. That's where
we're trying to do the same thing there.

00:11:16.248 --> 00:11:18.308
Yeah. I want to get to Janice in a second.

00:11:18.708 --> 00:11:20.788
I have a really hard-hitting
two-parter here.

00:11:20.948 --> 00:11:24.028
One, when we were talking
before we got going here,

00:11:24.188 --> 00:11:26.428
you told me a really
interesting fact about Summa.

00:11:26.448 --> 00:11:28.348
So can you share that with us again?

00:11:28.368 --> 00:11:29.868
Because I thought that was
the coolest thing ever.

00:11:29.888 --> 00:11:31.828
Well, the three most interesting facts about

00:11:31.848 --> 00:11:34.228
Summa, first of all, LeBron
James and Steph Curry

00:11:35.048 --> 00:11:35.748
were born there.

00:11:36.048 --> 00:11:39.088
And then Alcoholics Anonymous
was actually started at Summa.

00:11:39.228 --> 00:11:41.728
And so I think it's actually, we forget

00:11:41.748 --> 00:11:44.848
these institutions that
are around for 100 years.

00:11:45.548 --> 00:11:48.908
Those towns were really vibrant,
and they were very innovative. And

00:11:49.688 --> 00:11:53.448
just because of the tax of the way
the healthcare industry developed,

00:11:53.668 --> 00:11:56.848
these companies that are still the
largest employers have just become

00:11:57.748 --> 00:11:58.948
economically diminished-

00:11:58.968 --> 00:11:59.468
Yeah...

00:11:59.388 --> 00:12:02.728
as businesses. But there was a lot
of great legacy in these places,

00:12:02.748 --> 00:12:07.048
and so a lot of our focus is on can
we take an institution like that,

00:12:07.148 --> 00:12:11.368
bring in our set of founders, and
take that institution to the future

00:12:11.688 --> 00:12:15.108
in the way they should be delivering
care to take care of their community.

00:12:15.548 --> 00:12:17.248
And when you talk about the metrics

00:12:17.968 --> 00:12:19.828
Obviously it's going to be both, I'm sure,

00:12:19.988 --> 00:12:22.288
patient care-based metrics
and economic metrics.

00:12:22.308 --> 00:12:25.468
What are the two or three most
important, and how have they moved?

00:12:26.348 --> 00:12:28.868
If I was to think about just healthcare,
I assume that's what you're asking?

00:12:28.888 --> 00:12:29.388
Yeah. In summary.

00:12:29.308 --> 00:12:30.328
So, I mean, there's a few things.

00:12:30.348 --> 00:12:34.028
One is, because of the way the system
is set up and there's no trust between

00:12:34.048 --> 00:12:39.348
insurance companies and providers, hospitals
think insurance companies don't pay enough.

00:12:39.508 --> 00:12:41.208
Insurance companies think
hospitals bill too much.

00:12:41.228 --> 00:12:41.728
Yeah.

00:12:41.788 --> 00:12:44.148
So there's these armies
stood up on both sides.

00:12:44.168 --> 00:12:48.228
There's this enormous work tax in an
industry that already is bankrupting us.

00:12:48.368 --> 00:12:51.328
First thing is, can you just make
that go away and create sort of

00:12:51.348 --> 00:12:55.088
an AI-based system that just eliminates
how the payments really flow?

00:12:55.108 --> 00:12:55.608
Mm-hmm.

00:12:55.228 --> 00:12:56.148
That's an example.

00:12:56.448 --> 00:12:57.428
The second thing is

00:12:58.368 --> 00:13:03.948
the burnout of the workforces, which
was already pretty bad given the EHRs

00:13:04.048 --> 00:13:07.968
and the way we digitized the industry,
then got extremely bad because of COVID.

00:13:08.368 --> 00:13:09.768
So that workforce is burnt out.

00:13:09.788 --> 00:13:12.088
It's actually, in fact, the
reason why healthcare is the

00:13:12.108 --> 00:13:15.768
fastest adopter of AI, because
the workforce is so burnt out.

00:13:15.788 --> 00:13:17.668
We need to get them so they can get their

00:13:17.688 --> 00:13:22.848
bearings and can actually take care of the
people. And we're not triaging resources.

00:13:22.948 --> 00:13:26.268
We triage a nurse capacity
to put it on the sickest

00:13:26.288 --> 00:13:28.308
people as opposed to we should
have abundance around it.

00:13:28.328 --> 00:13:28.828
Hmm.

00:13:28.448 --> 00:13:30.988
So that's a really interesting
and important leverage.

00:13:31.148 --> 00:13:32.928
And the third is the patient experience.

00:13:33.228 --> 00:13:36.888
How do you actually make it be
that they can navigate the system

00:13:37.128 --> 00:13:40.268
and they can understand and take
care of themselves proactively?

00:13:40.928 --> 00:13:44.048
Because the best hospital
system should be one where the

00:13:44.068 --> 00:13:47.488
economics create the dynamics
that nobody comes to it, right?

00:13:47.508 --> 00:13:48.008
Right.

00:13:48.188 --> 00:13:49.848
People want health. People don't want

00:13:50.628 --> 00:13:55.648
sick care. And so these levers that
move these dimensions are-- and

00:13:55.668 --> 00:13:59.848
again, no single company can do
that, but this ecosystem of founders

00:13:59.868 --> 00:14:02.028
that we're working with together
can actually go solve that problem.

00:14:02.048 --> 00:14:05.908
That's cool. How does it work? Maybe walk
us through the Janus example as well.

00:14:06.728 --> 00:14:09.868
Look, Janus is another
100-year-old institution.

00:14:09.888 --> 00:14:11.588
It's got about half a
trillion dollars in managers.

00:14:11.608 --> 00:14:14.588
It's got 65 million retail
users all over the world.

00:14:15.108 --> 00:14:15.708
And

00:14:16.528 --> 00:14:19.168
what do you think is, if you think about the

00:14:19.188 --> 00:14:21.988
world of haves and the
have-nots, these retail

00:14:24.168 --> 00:14:26.828
market, the consumers don't have access
to what's going on in our world.

00:14:26.848 --> 00:14:27.808
So the first thing is,

00:14:28.708 --> 00:14:31.088
can you democratize opportunity for them?

00:14:31.228 --> 00:14:35.348
Can you change the experience and
increase their sophistication using AI?

00:14:35.748 --> 00:14:37.448
And that's sort of the way you're going to

00:14:37.468 --> 00:14:39.388
transform the experience
of the end customer.

00:14:39.848 --> 00:14:41.108
But obviously, in order to do all that, you

00:14:41.128 --> 00:14:43.088
first have to make Janus itself AI native.

00:14:43.108 --> 00:14:43.608
Hmm.

00:14:43.548 --> 00:14:46.668
So again, Percepta, our AI
team, is working with them to

00:14:47.088 --> 00:14:50.688
operationalize sort of an AI-based
operating system at Janus.

00:14:50.708 --> 00:14:53.588
And then we start to think
about innovating so that their

00:14:53.608 --> 00:14:56.928
customers can have abundance of
opportunity where they can invest.

00:14:57.188 --> 00:15:00.268
And so I think it's another example of a

00:15:00.288 --> 00:15:03.308
living lab where we can
say, just like what is the

00:15:04.068 --> 00:15:05.928
health experience consumers should have?

00:15:05.948 --> 00:15:08.248
What is the wealth experience
consumers should have in this next era?

00:15:08.348 --> 00:15:10.848
Yeah. Super. And does that feed back in?

00:15:10.868 --> 00:15:13.068
Does that impact the cost of
capital for the businesses that

00:15:13.088 --> 00:15:15.808
you're talking about when you're
going to be a capital entrepreneur?

00:15:16.088 --> 00:15:17.488
Is that feeding back in and kind of lowering

00:15:17.508 --> 00:15:19.448
their cost of capital and
helping them scale as well?

00:15:19.468 --> 00:15:23.008
Well, look, I think there's definitely
flywheel effects for us because

00:15:24.068 --> 00:15:29.168
if we have the economic leverage in asset

00:15:29.188 --> 00:15:31.628
management with a platform
like this, we can actually go

00:15:32.428 --> 00:15:35.328
scale the platform, buy more asset managers.

00:15:35.348 --> 00:15:35.848
Hmm.

00:15:35.788 --> 00:15:36.868
If you think about

00:15:37.668 --> 00:15:39.708
where's that capital going,
it could actually be made

00:15:39.728 --> 00:15:42.208
available to the capital
entrepreneurs in our industry.

00:15:42.348 --> 00:15:42.848
Yeah.

00:15:42.428 --> 00:15:44.688
And if those capital
entrepreneurs are accessing this

00:15:44.708 --> 00:15:48.208
capital, then by default,
they're giving these consumers

00:15:48.228 --> 00:15:51.188
access to our industry where so much
opportunity is getting created.

00:15:51.208 --> 00:15:54.348
And so I think that's very
top of mind for us is, in

00:15:54.368 --> 00:15:57.788
this world of concentration,
how do we really diffuse

00:15:58.948 --> 00:16:02.248
opportunity and create prosperity
for everybody? Because

00:16:03.688 --> 00:16:04.808
I don't think we're going to

00:16:06.228 --> 00:16:08.848
be allowed to do it any
other way by society.

00:16:08.868 --> 00:16:09.368
Hmm.

00:16:12.028 --> 00:16:15.028
I think there's something very interesting
about this argument that in order to

00:16:15.048 --> 00:16:19.188
fight the concentration, you need to concentrate
and then work on behalf of everybody.

00:16:19.208 --> 00:16:20.928
Is that kind of how you think about

00:16:21.928 --> 00:16:23.968
GC's role in what's going on here?

00:16:24.008 --> 00:16:27.248
Well, I don't think we are
focused on trying to concentrate.

00:16:27.268 --> 00:16:27.768
Hmm.

00:16:27.688 --> 00:16:33.388
I think we're trying to create
conditions for founders to

00:16:33.408 --> 00:16:36.308
become big enough, then as an
ecosystem, that is sort of

00:16:36.328 --> 00:16:39.108
the counter-concentration of
our architecture for innovation-

00:16:39.248 --> 00:16:39.748
Yeah...

00:16:39.287 --> 00:16:40.588
so that that can happen.

00:16:40.768 --> 00:16:44.188
If you are trying to innovate in the space

00:16:44.208 --> 00:16:49.048
industry today, think about
the kind of moat SpaceX has.

00:16:49.448 --> 00:16:49.948
Yeah.

00:16:50.068 --> 00:16:53.708
Do we think the space industry
should be run by one company?

00:16:54.248 --> 00:16:56.488
If not, what are we doing
to create conditions for

00:16:56.508 --> 00:17:00.148
founders to actually succeed and
democratize that opportunity?

00:17:01.208 --> 00:17:04.348
I'm obsessed with this. We
have to solve these problems.

00:17:04.768 --> 00:17:08.168
I really admire the folks that have
created this kind of concentration.

00:17:08.188 --> 00:17:10.228
Again, we're investing in them.

00:17:10.568 --> 00:17:15.468
But part of what makes capitalism work
for society is a level playing field-

00:17:15.488 --> 00:17:15.988
Right...

00:17:15.508 --> 00:17:17.128
where new companies can
actually continue to emerge.

00:17:17.148 --> 00:17:18.888
I mean, that's for you back as well.

00:17:20.088 --> 00:17:24.428
And are we really making that infeasible in

00:17:24.448 --> 00:17:26.408
today's environment, and how
do we really challenge that?

00:17:26.428 --> 00:17:31.348
So, what do we need to have in terms
of scale to create those conditions?

00:17:32.328 --> 00:17:35.768
That's the scale, that's the
criteria for where we're focused.

00:17:35.788 --> 00:17:38.228
Yeah. You mentioned being
in some of those companies.

00:17:38.388 --> 00:17:40.588
I thought one of the most interesting,
as I was doing my research for

00:17:40.608 --> 00:17:43.268
this, I was listening to you were
on "Invest Like the Best", great

00:17:43.288 --> 00:17:46.608
conversation with Patrick, and
you said something that I strongly

00:17:46.628 --> 00:17:51.328
agreed with, which was, "We don't
want to play the game of-- It's not

00:17:51.348 --> 00:17:55.908
our game to play to invest in the model
layer where they're going for AGI,

00:17:56.628 --> 00:17:58.928
because we don't think the ROI
is going to be there because

00:17:58.948 --> 00:18:01.528
these models are going to
commoditize, and that goes away."

00:18:01.668 --> 00:18:04.548
And then pretty quickly, and I
respect this, you turned around

00:18:04.568 --> 00:18:06.488
and you're like, "Actually,
Anthropic is a hell of a business."

00:18:06.708 --> 00:18:09.728
What changed between that and it
becoming your biggest investment?

00:18:09.748 --> 00:18:10.428
Yeah. So,

00:18:11.668 --> 00:18:12.688
I actually think

00:18:13.428 --> 00:18:15.288
the long-term view of

00:18:16.108 --> 00:18:20.048
the economic infrastructure of AI that
needs to power society hasn't changed.

00:18:20.068 --> 00:18:20.568
Hmm.

00:18:20.448 --> 00:18:21.728
So I would say,

00:18:22.508 --> 00:18:24.468
if you think about the continuum, you've got

00:18:24.488 --> 00:18:28.256
these frontier labs that are
continuing to push-... the edge-

00:18:28.276 --> 00:18:28.776
Right...

00:18:28.416 --> 00:18:30.536
in what's possible, the
level of intelligence.

00:18:30.696 --> 00:18:33.036
But every time there's a new
model, but any one of them,

00:18:34.256 --> 00:18:37.796
there's a certain amount of workloads
in society that don't need more

00:18:37.816 --> 00:18:42.096
intelligence, and I think we're like
probably 90%, 95% of the work that

00:18:42.116 --> 00:18:46.416
needs to be done that most people do in society
to where the knowledge work can be done-

00:18:46.496 --> 00:18:46.996
Yeah...

00:18:46.636 --> 00:18:47.536
with the models today.

00:18:48.136 --> 00:18:52.956
So, I think breaking that
frontier to open new frontiers in

00:18:52.976 --> 00:18:56.936
research, and having a company that
enables that is very important.

00:18:57.416 --> 00:19:00.956
I also think for markets where
strategic autonomy matters for different

00:19:00.976 --> 00:19:05.416
countries, they're going to want to
have sovereign model capabilities-

00:19:05.436 --> 00:19:05.936
Yeah...

00:19:05.536 --> 00:19:07.196
that enable it, so I think
that's really important.

00:19:07.476 --> 00:19:09.656
But when I think about an enterprise,

00:19:10.396 --> 00:19:14.276
and you look at all the token spend
and what's happening today, I can't

00:19:14.296 --> 00:19:17.216
imagine a world where it's going to be
that we're standardizing on one of these.

00:19:17.236 --> 00:19:18.796
I think you're going to have,
and this is why we invested in

00:19:18.816 --> 00:19:22.836
Cognition, I think you want to help
these companies figure out how

00:19:22.856 --> 00:19:26.696
to navigate, shard their workloads
into where do we need the

00:19:26.716 --> 00:19:29.716
sophisticated frontier capabilities
like Anthropic, and where is

00:19:29.736 --> 00:19:33.756
open source good enough, and what's in between,
and what can we build ourselves, right?

00:19:33.776 --> 00:19:35.656
So I think you're going
to have this continuum.

00:19:35.676 --> 00:19:36.176
Yeah.

00:19:35.896 --> 00:19:38.716
And so that infrastructure,
there's a place for open source.

00:19:38.736 --> 00:19:41.116
We invested in Mistral, so
we're deep believers in that

00:19:41.136 --> 00:19:44.396
ecosystem needs to exist, and
that this is going to exist.

00:19:44.776 --> 00:19:50.016
What I did not realize at that time
was the pace with which we're going to

00:19:50.776 --> 00:19:53.176
concentrate. And then yesterday, I was

00:19:53.196 --> 00:19:56.436
actually speaking at an
event, and it just hit me.

00:19:56.456 --> 00:19:58.736
I'm like, "Of course this
was going to happen."

00:19:58.836 --> 00:20:02.676
I've been talking about it for a while,
saying this is the first technology where

00:20:02.696 --> 00:20:06.516
every CEO in every industry in every country
is thinking about it at the same time.

00:20:06.656 --> 00:20:08.976
Well, if that's the
dynamic, then every one of

00:20:08.996 --> 00:20:11.056
them is going to experiment
with it at the same time.

00:20:11.076 --> 00:20:13.296
They're going to figure out
their use cases at the same time.

00:20:13.416 --> 00:20:18.096
So you're going to see unprecedented
growth, and that's really what we're seeing.

00:20:19.256 --> 00:20:21.456
It hit the zeitgeist like no
other technology before has.

00:20:21.476 --> 00:20:22.936
It's wild to see.

00:20:22.956 --> 00:20:26.376
And I think you're probably the
perfect person to be talking to

00:20:26.396 --> 00:20:29.416
about this, because it's just
something that if that happens

00:20:29.656 --> 00:20:32.876
where they're making the
investment and the investments are

00:20:32.896 --> 00:20:37.596
getting bigger and bigger to push the
frontier, but that covers less and less

00:20:38.636 --> 00:20:41.436
of the economic work, like you
get diminishing returns on that,

00:20:42.076 --> 00:20:45.996
what's the economic case for an
Anthropic as they continue to go?

00:20:46.316 --> 00:20:47.016
This is

00:20:48.296 --> 00:20:50.836
a very hard question to
answer, and I can tell

00:20:50.856 --> 00:20:53.756
it's also a very hard
question for Dario to answer.

00:20:53.976 --> 00:20:54.496
Yeah.

00:20:55.276 --> 00:20:58.016
I'm projecting here, but the reality is,

00:20:58.916 --> 00:21:01.156
if they said, "We're going
to be just a platform

00:21:02.296 --> 00:21:05.716
and provide this intelligence, but
it gets commoditized by the open

00:21:05.736 --> 00:21:08.676
source," then as he's been saying,
unless we get to a trillion

00:21:08.696 --> 00:21:11.696
dollars revenue, we can't have the
infrastructure that can power these models.

00:21:11.996 --> 00:21:14.696
So that maybe won't get him to scale.

00:21:14.876 --> 00:21:15.376
Yep.

00:21:15.376 --> 00:21:20.756
So what's the next step? Figure out what
profit pools you want to go capture up top.

00:21:21.156 --> 00:21:23.656
And when we invested at the
$60 billion valuation, the bet

00:21:23.676 --> 00:21:27.356
was that Claude was going to be
their app, sort of in reverse.

00:21:27.496 --> 00:21:29.376
Usually, you have a killer app,
and then you become a platform.

00:21:29.396 --> 00:21:30.056
They were a platform.

00:21:30.076 --> 00:21:33.356
They actually, in one of those rare
cases, figured out a killer app.

00:21:33.376 --> 00:21:33.876
Yeah.

00:21:34.136 --> 00:21:36.836
Then they've gone and looked at,
"Hey, should we do stuff in legal?"

00:21:36.856 --> 00:21:40.796
And given his biology background, they
want to do stuff in life sciences.

00:21:40.816 --> 00:21:46.916
So the challenge, I think for these companies
as they migrate to have enough profit

00:21:46.936 --> 00:21:51.576
pool to sustain and feed the beast, so to
speak, so they can actually survive and

00:21:51.596 --> 00:21:56.756
provide the good that they can provide, is
at odds with how a platform should behave.

00:21:57.216 --> 00:22:00.356
And I think they need to
figure out where is it enough

00:22:00.376 --> 00:22:03.696
where they can then also democratize
innovation in other parts.

00:22:03.716 --> 00:22:08.496
And so I can tell the
company's in that, dealing

00:22:08.516 --> 00:22:10.876
with that tension real
time to figure this out.

00:22:11.136 --> 00:22:15.316
Yeah. My optimistic take
is that you have what

00:22:15.336 --> 00:22:19.136
Isomorphic is doing, what Anthropic
might do in life sciences.

00:22:19.156 --> 00:22:22.976
You have what Chai Discovery's doing. You
have a bunch of stuff happening there.

00:22:22.996 --> 00:22:25.876
I have no idea how you judge
who wins that other than

00:22:25.896 --> 00:22:28.296
talent, but there's a lot of
really good talent on every team.

00:22:28.536 --> 00:22:30.016
I just think this feels like
it's going to be one of the

00:22:30.036 --> 00:22:33.156
greatest consumer surplus events
in the history of the world.

00:22:33.176 --> 00:22:35.016
It's not going to go kill us.

00:22:35.036 --> 00:22:37.276
It's not going to whatever,
but you just have a lot of

00:22:37.296 --> 00:22:39.516
really smart people competing
to deliver the best product,

00:22:39.536 --> 00:22:42.236
but hopefully, they compete to the
point where the prices are good.

00:22:42.256 --> 00:22:44.556
Look, I share your optimism. At Sumo,

00:22:45.395 --> 00:22:46.536
one of the companies we incubated was

00:22:46.556 --> 00:22:50.796
Hippocratic, which it's
got a nurse that is 10X

00:22:51.976 --> 00:22:54.236
lower price than a human nurse, an agentic

00:22:54.256 --> 00:22:57.456
nurse, and they can change
their care models with it.

00:22:58.096 --> 00:23:00.156
The fact that all these
people can get care in their

00:23:00.176 --> 00:23:03.096
home that they weren't
before, that's what AI is for.

00:23:03.116 --> 00:23:04.916
I think people need to
touch and feel this in their

00:23:04.936 --> 00:23:07.616
daily lives so they're less
scared of it, to your point.

00:23:07.636 --> 00:23:08.136
Yeah.

00:23:08.176 --> 00:23:10.436
And this innovation that you're describing,

00:23:10.936 --> 00:23:14.216
what is it going to do for drug
discovery? Let's just talk about it.

00:23:14.716 --> 00:23:18.256
Today, just like it used to
be in the movies, it has to

00:23:18.276 --> 00:23:21.276
be a blockbuster drug to be
worth any company doing it.

00:23:21.296 --> 00:23:21.796
Mm-hmm.

00:23:21.856 --> 00:23:22.916
Then we had Netflix.

00:23:23.536 --> 00:23:25.536
So I think you're going to
start seeing can we actually

00:23:25.556 --> 00:23:29.476
identify drugs that are
profitable to make and much

00:23:29.496 --> 00:23:32.276
higher efficacy for smaller and
smaller segments of populations.

00:23:32.596 --> 00:23:34.576
That'll only happen because you get AI

00:23:35.356 --> 00:23:36.476
to work, and obviously, the

00:23:37.316 --> 00:23:39.276
regulatory processes will
have to change for this.

00:23:39.296 --> 00:23:43.036
It's not as simple as just
AI showing up in this space.

00:23:43.176 --> 00:23:43.676
Yes.

00:23:43.196 --> 00:23:46.736
I think people underestimate that.
But notwithstanding that particular

00:23:47.596 --> 00:23:50.916
complexity, I think it's going
to be phenomenal, and it's going

00:23:50.936 --> 00:23:54.016
to further change that healthcare
transformation because that's

00:23:54.036 --> 00:23:56.716
not going to really be complete
until we also transform pharma.

00:23:56.836 --> 00:23:57.336
Yeah.

00:23:57.196 --> 00:24:00.956
In every industry, you have an opportunity
like this. So I share your optimism.

00:24:00.976 --> 00:24:04.456
I'm very excited about the
abundance we can create.

00:24:04.636 --> 00:24:09.516
My worry is just if all this abundance
is provided by a handful of companies

00:24:10.236 --> 00:24:15.256
and we're losing agency as a society, as
individuals, that's not going to feel good.

00:24:15.536 --> 00:24:18.196
I don't think that's the world that any
of us want to live in, so we just have to

00:24:18.216 --> 00:24:24.156
figure out how does abundance come with
also abundant opportunity for everybody.

00:24:24.436 --> 00:24:25.336
And how do you think about it?

00:24:25.356 --> 00:24:28.436
Because I know you, in this
letter, wrote about the

00:24:28.456 --> 00:24:31.476
causes of the tech lash, and
I really like the framing of

00:24:31.856 --> 00:24:35.176
having AI being done to them as
opposed to doing it themselves.

00:24:35.648 --> 00:24:38.348
How do you think that you solve for that?

00:24:39.268 --> 00:24:44.408
Look, as I said, my big thing is
agency. I think this, where the

00:24:45.548 --> 00:24:49.528
concentration companies will say,
"You're not going to need money, and

00:24:49.548 --> 00:24:52.388
there's going to be all this abundance, and
it's all just going to be given to you."

00:24:52.948 --> 00:24:54.088
I wrote this

00:24:55.608 --> 00:24:59.508
piece seven years ago where I
said, "What if it all comes true?"

00:24:59.748 --> 00:25:02.608
And I was like, in the Valley,
there were three experiments going

00:25:02.628 --> 00:25:06.368
on at the time, self-driving
trucks, longevity, and basic income.

00:25:06.868 --> 00:25:09.828
I was like, they're all great ideas
on their own, but when it all comes

00:25:09.848 --> 00:25:12.888
together, you're going to go to five
million people that are truck drivers,

00:25:12.908 --> 00:25:16.948
it was four at that time, and you're
going to tell them, "Good news, bad news.

00:25:17.108 --> 00:25:19.008
You're going to live 40 years longer.

00:25:19.508 --> 00:25:20.608
Your job's being taken away.

00:25:20.628 --> 00:25:22.108
And by the way, we're going to put you on a

00:25:22.128 --> 00:25:24.508
stipend, so your dignity's
being taken away."

00:25:24.528 --> 00:25:25.028
Yeah.

00:25:25.388 --> 00:25:29.148
I just think we almost have to
think about Silicon Valley as

00:25:30.128 --> 00:25:34.308
a unified ecosystem, almost as a
company, and say we're creating an

00:25:34.328 --> 00:25:38.748
offering for society, and is that
the offering that people really want?

00:25:38.848 --> 00:25:41.307
And is that where they're
going to be the happiest?

00:25:41.688 --> 00:25:44.508
I am at odds with the existing
philosophy in the Valley of just, "Oh,

00:25:44.528 --> 00:25:46.748
we're just going to go vertically
integrate and do all these things.

00:25:46.768 --> 00:25:49.368
It's going to be great for people." No.
People need to feel like they did it.

00:25:49.608 --> 00:25:52.268
People need to have
opportunities for themselves.

00:25:52.968 --> 00:25:53.608
You can't

00:25:54.948 --> 00:25:58.628
melt all the labor in every
country and take away people's

00:25:58.648 --> 00:26:01.568
work and turn it into a subscription
for a handful of companies.

00:26:02.088 --> 00:26:02.588
Yeah.

00:26:02.208 --> 00:26:04.448
Americans don't want that, let
alone the rest of the globe.

00:26:04.608 --> 00:26:07.968
So I think we just need to
understand architecturally what

00:26:08.028 --> 00:26:10.668
transformation are we really trying
to do and be intentional about it.

00:26:10.688 --> 00:26:14.848
And right now, it's very focused
on just getting to scale and

00:26:14.868 --> 00:26:18.508
sort of sucking up the profit pools,
because that's what capitalism-

00:26:18.708 --> 00:26:19.208
Yeah...

00:26:18.908 --> 00:26:21.668
sort of does. But at some point,
you do end up having too-big-to-fail

00:26:22.288 --> 00:26:24.828
dynamics, and I think that's sort of
where we're headed a little bit in tech.

00:26:24.888 --> 00:26:30.108
It does feel like the market has some
consciousness or something where

00:26:30.928 --> 00:26:33.188
Anthropic, I think, was
just running away with it.

00:26:33.248 --> 00:26:35.928
And then the market said,
we have Kimi K3 now.

00:26:36.868 --> 00:26:40.648
Americans are now cheering for Chinese
open source models because they're

00:26:40.668 --> 00:26:43.708
open, and if you ask it a question
about biology, it's going to answer your

00:26:43.728 --> 00:26:48.448
question about biology, whereas Anthropic's
going to kick you to a different model.

00:26:48.948 --> 00:26:51.268
It just feels like there's
this animal spirit that

00:26:51.288 --> 00:26:54.168
kind of fights against this
concentration a little bit.

00:26:54.788 --> 00:26:58.008
Look, I think it's very real. Capital
allocators have a role to play in this, right?

00:26:58.028 --> 00:26:58.528
Yeah.

00:26:58.108 --> 00:27:00.728
What are the kinds of things
we're seeking to actually fund?

00:27:01.108 --> 00:27:06.088
And we're very focused on how does the
US open source ecosystem become vibrant?

00:27:06.108 --> 00:27:08.048
Europe needs its own,
and China needs its own,

00:27:08.068 --> 00:27:09.988
because I think sovereignty's
going to matter.

00:27:10.008 --> 00:27:10.508
Yeah.

00:27:10.288 --> 00:27:11.748
And you also want to...

00:27:11.768 --> 00:27:15.808
One of the reasons we do a lot of work
in policy is you want to see all these

00:27:15.828 --> 00:27:18.248
nation states figure out how to create
their own infrastructure, because if

00:27:18.268 --> 00:27:21.388
we're going to melt labor into productivity,
they need to capture that productivity

00:27:22.268 --> 00:27:24.948
on their soil so that it can
be reinvested back in people's

00:27:24.968 --> 00:27:30.768
prosperity, as opposed to that it's going
to fly out to a company somewhere else.

00:27:31.128 --> 00:27:34.848
And so those conditions are worth

00:27:34.868 --> 00:27:37.528
understanding, so what we're
building is actually resilient

00:27:38.728 --> 00:27:41.108
AI infrastructure in the next 10, 20 years.

00:27:41.128 --> 00:27:43.248
So it's sort of an amazing
time to be figuring this out.

00:27:43.448 --> 00:27:47.008
Do you think it's, or when you're
talking to people in other

00:27:47.028 --> 00:27:50.848
countries that have a GDP that's
a fraction of what the United

00:27:50.868 --> 00:27:54.588
States is, do they have the
resources to build the compute, to

00:27:54.608 --> 00:27:57.348
build the infrastructure that it
requires to train their own models?

00:27:58.288 --> 00:28:01.468
You're a country with a GDP,
call it 5% of the US's.

00:28:01.488 --> 00:28:04.188
What are you doing to solve this problem?

00:28:04.708 --> 00:28:05.868
So I was in Greece yesterday.

00:28:06.028 --> 00:28:06.528
Yep.

00:28:06.268 --> 00:28:09.868
And I sat down with the prime minister
and talked about exactly this.

00:28:09.888 --> 00:28:11.468
So they're building their factories.

00:28:11.488 --> 00:28:13.948
They're trying to figure out,
they've centralized their healthcare

00:28:13.968 --> 00:28:18.328
data, so they can actually understand
how to use AI and intelligence.

00:28:18.568 --> 00:28:19.968
We walked them through some
of the things we're doing

00:28:19.988 --> 00:28:23.728
here, and their immediate reaction
was, "Hey, how can we do it?

00:28:24.168 --> 00:28:26.168
Can we do some pilots here
to learn how to do that?

00:28:26.188 --> 00:28:29.148
And what are we going to do in a way so
it actually gets done here?" Because

00:28:29.868 --> 00:28:33.288
what is the reason entrepreneurs
build great companies?

00:28:33.328 --> 00:28:34.888
Because they have empathy for the problem.

00:28:35.408 --> 00:28:39.728
So who's going to transform the
healthcare experience with AI in Greece?

00:28:39.748 --> 00:28:41.908
It's going to be somebody who lives
there, who's got empathy for it.

00:28:41.928 --> 00:28:45.628
So I do sort of just encouraging
these nation states to say,

00:28:45.648 --> 00:28:49.608
"Create those conditions where
you are setting up this compute

00:28:49.628 --> 00:28:53.247
infrastructure and an intelligence
ecosystem so that your local

00:28:53.528 --> 00:28:57.528
entrepreneurial ecosystem can build on
it," is something we deeply believe in.

00:28:57.548 --> 00:29:00.988
I don't think we want to solve the
world's problems out of Silicon Valley.

00:29:01.008 --> 00:29:04.168
We want to create the
technologies that others can

00:29:04.188 --> 00:29:06.568
harness so that they can be
resilient in their own way.

00:29:06.588 --> 00:29:09.948
And yeah, there's some
economic rent you capture, but

00:29:10.768 --> 00:29:12.388
it really does need to be that you level the

00:29:12.408 --> 00:29:15.028
playing field for entrepreneurship
to be much more global.

00:29:15.048 --> 00:29:17.148
It's sort of part of our global
resilience thesis, the way we've

00:29:17.168 --> 00:29:21.268
been operating, and why we're so
focused on investing in Europe,

00:29:21.288 --> 00:29:25.528
investing in India, investing in
MENA, all the allied nation states.

00:29:25.548 --> 00:29:29.828
There's some of my favorite hard tech
founders are in the GC portfolio.

00:29:29.848 --> 00:29:32.648
I wrote a piece with
Jordan at Zenra Systems-

00:29:32.668 --> 00:29:33.168
Oh, yeah...

00:29:32.968 --> 00:29:33.468
yesterday.

00:29:33.468 --> 00:29:33.968
Yeah. He's amazing.

00:29:33.668 --> 00:29:34.888
In Alex's introduction.

00:29:34.908 --> 00:29:40.648
He's awesome. Two of them, Anil Varanesi
and Evan Beard, both have said that

00:29:40.668 --> 00:29:45.088
you push them to think just much bigger
than other people that they work with.

00:29:45.108 --> 00:29:49.328
What does that look like, maybe outside
of the AI context, in a hardware context?

00:29:49.348 --> 00:29:51.368
What is thinking a lot bigger
for a founder at that stage?

00:29:51.388 --> 00:29:54.608
Look, I think it really does
come down to Evan's case.

00:29:54.628 --> 00:29:58.208
He's building a great company, Sandbot.
It's like he's really ripping.

00:29:59.088 --> 00:30:00.708
But I'm like, "What do
you really want to do?

00:30:00.728 --> 00:30:02.608
Do you want to go win this
market, or do you want to be the

00:30:02.628 --> 00:30:05.408
defining company that really
shapes robotics for the world?"

00:30:06.148 --> 00:30:08.828
There's no right or wrong. It's sort
of all about your personal ambition.

00:30:09.188 --> 00:30:11.148
But if you want to go...

00:30:11.668 --> 00:30:12.568
Because there's an opportunity.

00:30:12.588 --> 00:30:15.368
Some company or some set of companies
are going to shape what robotics is going

00:30:15.388 --> 00:30:19.408
to look like, and we're going to go from
driving automation in our industrial

00:30:19.428 --> 00:30:23.788
settings to creating such amazing technology
that factories get built around it.

00:30:24.588 --> 00:30:25.368
That'll be the future.

00:30:25.388 --> 00:30:25.888
Yeah.

00:30:26.048 --> 00:30:29.188
If you really want to do
that, then you've got to have

00:30:29.928 --> 00:30:31.828
a long-term vision you're
building towards, and make

00:30:31.848 --> 00:30:33.888
sure your short-term decisions
are aligning with it.

00:30:34.148 --> 00:30:37.680
So just pushing, because you
become so myopic And it is

00:30:37.700 --> 00:30:41.000
such a difficult time right
now because everything keeps

00:30:41.020 --> 00:30:44.460
changing around us, so you keep
having to retest your assumptions.

00:30:44.700 --> 00:30:48.180
And there's so much strain on everybody
on, like, "Am I doing the right things?

00:30:48.240 --> 00:30:50.200
Am I in the most important areas?

00:30:50.960 --> 00:30:52.140
Did I miss something?

00:30:52.780 --> 00:30:54.120
Is there something else
that's going to come?"

00:30:54.140 --> 00:30:57.880
Because there is this inherent second-mover
advantage in this world right now-

00:30:57.900 --> 00:30:58.400
Yeah...

00:30:57.920 --> 00:30:59.580
that's just going to come sideswipe me,

00:30:59.900 --> 00:31:02.160
that you don't really
get time to think about

00:31:02.940 --> 00:31:04.960
what long-term, what is this
world going to look like?

00:31:05.080 --> 00:31:08.840
And so just pausing them to make
the time to think about that

00:31:08.860 --> 00:31:12.320
and say, "Are we making the right
set of choices to get there?"

00:31:12.640 --> 00:31:14.600
That's a place where I generally am

00:31:16.260 --> 00:31:16.880
trying to

00:31:17.880 --> 00:31:20.520
push them. And then we have some great
conversations, both of them, about this.

00:31:21.280 --> 00:31:24.600
If we're doing this conversation
in a quarter or two,

00:31:25.520 --> 00:31:28.800
and everything's moving so fast right now,
what do you think has changed the most?

00:31:28.820 --> 00:31:30.400
Or what is something that
you believe will change the

00:31:30.420 --> 00:31:33.300
most that you disagree most
with the rest of the market on?

00:31:34.480 --> 00:31:38.740
I don't know if I disagree
with the rest of the market

00:31:39.560 --> 00:31:44.100
on this or not, but the ability
to scale these things and the

00:31:44.120 --> 00:31:47.840
importance of getting the public
sentiment right, I think that

00:31:48.700 --> 00:31:49.500
is coming.

00:31:49.580 --> 00:31:50.080
Yeah.

00:31:49.720 --> 00:31:54.360
And I've spent time with folks
on both sides of the aisle, and

00:31:55.320 --> 00:31:59.860
other than China, that's the one thing
they agree on, that people are scared.

00:31:59.880 --> 00:32:03.940
And I think this is becoming more of
a hindrance that has to navigate.

00:32:03.960 --> 00:32:07.260
Think about what's happening in the US.
Let's take the US for a second, right?

00:32:07.560 --> 00:32:09.760
US has made a venture capital
bet on infrastructure.

00:32:10.400 --> 00:32:13.900
And we have all this capital that
has gone down into projects.

00:32:14.240 --> 00:32:18.440
And do you know how much equity can
get wiped out if these companies

00:32:19.520 --> 00:32:20.680
don't hit their timelines-

00:32:20.700 --> 00:32:21.200
Yeah...

00:32:20.880 --> 00:32:22.200
of developing infrastructure?

00:32:22.520 --> 00:32:25.280
And guess what's coming from
the other side? There's real

00:32:26.040 --> 00:32:28.600
pressure on slowing things
down because of fear.

00:32:29.860 --> 00:32:32.180
I worry about that
creating a negative spiral

00:32:32.200 --> 00:32:37.880
that really hits us from a
momentum standpoint. And I think

00:32:39.760 --> 00:32:41.440
most people are not taking that seriously

00:32:41.460 --> 00:32:43.580
enough, and they think they
can just power through it.

00:32:43.600 --> 00:32:46.780
Some of the firms sort of engage
excessively in politics to think

00:32:46.800 --> 00:32:49.460
that's the answer, but I do think
it's about bringing people along.

00:32:49.640 --> 00:32:51.440
We're heavily underestimating that.

00:32:51.900 --> 00:32:52.400
Yeah.

00:32:54.520 --> 00:32:57.540
I write and tell stories for a living,
and it's so hard to figure out how to get

00:32:57.560 --> 00:33:03.420
this one right from where we are now because
I think we've gotten it so wrong so far.

00:33:03.500 --> 00:33:05.620
Where do you see AI ending up?

00:33:05.640 --> 00:33:07.120
Because I think there are
these two worlds where it's a

00:33:07.140 --> 00:33:10.740
tool and where it is this thing
that just does a lot of work.

00:33:11.680 --> 00:33:16.100
Where do you think we end up in short
term, kind of 10 years from now?

00:33:16.740 --> 00:33:17.520
What does that look like?

00:33:17.540 --> 00:33:19.680
Yeah, look, I think short term,
medium term, long term, right?

00:33:19.700 --> 00:33:23.760
So short term, I would say it really
is a tool. So my daughter just, she

00:33:24.540 --> 00:33:26.160
graduated, and she's working at an

00:33:27.160 --> 00:33:28.980
AI company. She's building
all these agents to

00:33:29.460 --> 00:33:31.400
automate things, and she's like,
"Oh, I can automate my job."

00:33:31.420 --> 00:33:34.880
And so right now it's all about
we're reimagining how we do

00:33:34.900 --> 00:33:38.060
our work, and I think it's going
to be longer than people think.

00:33:38.080 --> 00:33:41.840
I actually think, just as a slight
detour, somebody's going to

00:33:41.860 --> 00:33:44.120
start saying AGI is here, and
it's going to rattle the markets.

00:33:44.220 --> 00:33:45.960
But the diffusion of this
stuff is actually so much

00:33:45.980 --> 00:33:47.980
slower that it's going to be
way longer than people think.

00:33:48.000 --> 00:33:48.500
Yeah.

00:33:48.560 --> 00:33:53.240
But the next several years is about
humanity using it as a tool and

00:33:55.420 --> 00:33:56.980
making sure it drives productivity.

00:33:58.000 --> 00:33:59.260
Somebody said to me yesterday that there's a

00:33:59.280 --> 00:34:01.440
fine line between
productivity and complacency.

00:34:01.920 --> 00:34:05.500
And so we can learn how to actually
have it drive productivity.

00:34:05.720 --> 00:34:07.700
I think medium term, we're going to start

00:34:07.720 --> 00:34:13.960
imagining new ways of driving experiences
in society. So if a nurse is free,

00:34:14.720 --> 00:34:16.560
how would you take care of people
fundamentally differently?

00:34:16.580 --> 00:34:17.080
Hmm.

00:34:17.480 --> 00:34:19.580
That's going to lead to a whole
new set of jobs around that.

00:34:19.600 --> 00:34:22.300
Or if tutoring is going to,
or teaching is going to go

00:34:22.320 --> 00:34:24.000
from a four-year college
to, hey, it should just be

00:34:24.020 --> 00:34:27.100
lifelong learning, and you've got
this AI tutor that does a video.

00:34:27.120 --> 00:34:29.660
That's just one theory people
are experimenting with.

00:34:29.980 --> 00:34:31.300
Well, how would the education system change?

00:34:31.320 --> 00:34:34.940
So I think the second phase, which is
longer than people think, is probably that.

00:34:36.520 --> 00:34:41.840
And somewhere in there, we're going to
go from robotics where it is today to

00:34:41.920 --> 00:34:45.640
robotic models working, but also the supply
chains becoming sophisticated to adopt it.

00:34:45.660 --> 00:34:46.160
Yeah.

00:34:47.020 --> 00:34:51.720
But on a long, long-term basis, I
do worry about do we need to have a

00:34:52.160 --> 00:34:55.860
post-work society because all
cognitive and physical things will just

00:34:55.880 --> 00:34:59.420
be done better by machines for a large
percentage of things in the world. And so

00:35:00.440 --> 00:35:03.140
my main thing is we're
not going to fight it.

00:35:03.160 --> 00:35:06.420
Capital allocation eventually
will push through the resistance.

00:35:06.440 --> 00:35:07.660
We're not going to fight it.

00:35:09.100 --> 00:35:13.720
But we need a calm environment
as society to embrace this,

00:35:13.740 --> 00:35:16.860
see its benefits, appreciate
our agency in it, and then

00:35:16.880 --> 00:35:19.500
imagine what life really needs to
be look like for the next phase.

00:35:19.860 --> 00:35:25.440
And so rather than creating this anxiety
and making people afraid and sort of

00:35:25.640 --> 00:35:28.860
jamming it down people, if we can have
a better conditions in which this is

00:35:28.880 --> 00:35:33.420
getting adopted, so we also have a calm
society to think through these problems.

00:35:33.560 --> 00:35:35.880
That's a little bit of sort of what I
think a lot about on the longer arc.

00:35:35.900 --> 00:35:36.400
Yeah.

00:35:35.940 --> 00:35:41.120
Can we just buy ourselves 30 calm years
where we're making progress, we're

00:35:41.140 --> 00:35:45.140
letting people feel the benefits of it
in terms of agency and how it changes

00:35:45.160 --> 00:35:49.260
their lives, and give us the head space
to think about the longer-term issues?

00:35:50.280 --> 00:35:51.840
That to me is my

00:35:53.000 --> 00:35:55.160
dream scenario, is that maybe we can sort of

00:35:55.180 --> 00:35:57.160
mature as an ecosystem and
start behaving that way.

00:35:57.180 --> 00:35:59.380
Yeah. Who's responsible for that?

00:35:59.980 --> 00:36:02.920
I think Meta's done a pretty terrible job of

00:36:03.840 --> 00:36:06.560
communicating how they use
AI and fire people, and

00:36:06.580 --> 00:36:08.580
everybody's going to get
tracked at work, and we're going

00:36:08.600 --> 00:36:11.220
to use that to train models and
then probably fire more of you.

00:36:11.600 --> 00:36:15.800
Do CEOs have more of a role there to
take it on the chin in terms of margins?

00:36:15.820 --> 00:36:17.520
Or how does that actually-

00:36:17.800 --> 00:36:18.300
Yeah...

00:36:17.920 --> 00:36:20.160
because I agree with that idea.
How does it get into the economy?

00:36:20.180 --> 00:36:21.740
So look, I think look,

00:36:23.420 --> 00:36:25.760
there's zero to work,
there's a company, and then

00:36:25.780 --> 00:36:28.460
there's ecosystems and industries,
is the way I think about it.

00:36:28.860 --> 00:36:29.560
And

00:36:30.580 --> 00:36:33.640
we're going to go from this
humans and software making us

00:36:33.660 --> 00:36:37.760
productive to zero to work's
changing into what are we

00:36:37.780 --> 00:36:40.540
doing, what are agents doing, the
interplay in a new kind of an org.

00:36:41.040 --> 00:36:42.080
So the company changes.

00:36:42.100 --> 00:36:42.912
Yep

00:36:42.932 --> 00:36:45.052
Well, when that happens, you start thinking

00:36:45.072 --> 00:36:47.052
about how do I serve my
customers differently?

00:36:47.832 --> 00:36:48.352
And

00:36:49.912 --> 00:36:52.392
first step that's going to
happen is in every industry,

00:36:52.632 --> 00:36:55.392
it's not that AI makes those
markets become so much bigger.

00:36:55.472 --> 00:36:58.472
It's actually a game of
which company has the right

00:36:58.492 --> 00:37:01.892
leadership and the conditions
to take share from others,

00:37:02.792 --> 00:37:05.532
right? So the next five
to 10 years are just that

00:37:06.272 --> 00:37:08.292
reallocation of market share.

00:37:08.312 --> 00:37:08.812
Yeah.

00:37:09.072 --> 00:37:10.792
And that to me is all about the CEO.

00:37:11.632 --> 00:37:12.372
Do they

00:37:13.312 --> 00:37:16.692
inspire their team to say, "Hey, let's
do more with less," so you're not

00:37:16.712 --> 00:37:19.552
creating a fear of losing your job, so
here we're going to create more leverage.

00:37:19.752 --> 00:37:21.972
But that leverage allows you
to win share from others-

00:37:21.992 --> 00:37:22.492
Hmm...

00:37:23.012 --> 00:37:25.992
as opposed to not, and that
leverage allows you to then

00:37:26.012 --> 00:37:28.192
innovate to say, "Hey, we're
going to now be on this sort of

00:37:28.212 --> 00:37:31.112
highly innovative journey as well
to further take share from others."

00:37:31.792 --> 00:37:32.812
I think that's going to happen.

00:37:33.872 --> 00:37:38.332
In fact, when I look at Janus, Ali,
who runs it, is an amazing CEO.

00:37:38.352 --> 00:37:40.012
I actually think he's
capable of being able to do

00:37:40.032 --> 00:37:41.912
that in that industry, and
that's why we invested.

00:37:42.552 --> 00:37:46.152
It's very much a venture capital bet in
some ways in that regard because if we

00:37:46.172 --> 00:37:49.972
could create the AI transformation and
then scale, we'll take share from others.

00:37:49.992 --> 00:37:50.492
Hmm.

00:37:51.892 --> 00:37:55.052
Then the question is, okay,
once the next generation

00:37:55.072 --> 00:37:58.192
leaders that are more AI
native have been established,

00:37:58.452 --> 00:38:01.892
how's the industry going to change
because they have these new tools?

00:38:01.912 --> 00:38:03.472
The amount of work that is,

00:38:04.932 --> 00:38:06.712
I think it's very naive when
people say this stuff's

00:38:06.732 --> 00:38:08.832
going to change in two years
or three years or four years.

00:38:08.852 --> 00:38:11.072
You have to go inside of these companies.

00:38:11.312 --> 00:38:14.712
We have these living labs, as I said,
and I see what's really going on.

00:38:14.732 --> 00:38:16.152
It's going to be a while

00:38:17.292 --> 00:38:18.852
for these companies to really embrace.

00:38:18.932 --> 00:38:22.112
Yeah. Just a couple last questions.
You led perfectly in there.

00:38:23.092 --> 00:38:27.352
How has having the living labs changed
how you're investing on the venture side?

00:38:27.372 --> 00:38:33.312
Yeah. I mean, it's a great question.
So I think it just gives you

00:38:34.152 --> 00:38:38.352
so much better appreciation for the
end customers that our founders are

00:38:38.372 --> 00:38:42.472
selling into, and it also shows you what
the problems are that are not being solved.

00:38:42.492 --> 00:38:42.992
Hmm.

00:38:43.372 --> 00:38:47.992
So the magic really is the collaboration
between our venture investing

00:38:48.012 --> 00:38:52.412
team and the leaderships of these
labs and our AI transformation folks

00:38:52.432 --> 00:38:55.332
that are in these labs to say, "Hey,
how do we reframe these problems?

00:38:55.352 --> 00:38:58.392
How do we think about it? What's
missing? What needs to be built?"

00:38:58.732 --> 00:39:01.852
We'll go look for founders that
are working on that, and if

00:39:01.872 --> 00:39:04.692
they're not working on it, then
through our creation fund,

00:39:04.712 --> 00:39:06.832
we'll actually put a team together
and we'll go work on it ourselves.

00:39:06.852 --> 00:39:07.352
Yeah.

00:39:07.632 --> 00:39:11.192
So I think just being in that
knowledge flow and being real

00:39:11.212 --> 00:39:15.672
practitioners, real builders
alongside the founders, I think that

00:39:18.572 --> 00:39:21.512
it makes the work so much
more fun, and it's so much

00:39:21.532 --> 00:39:24.012
more grounded in reality as
to what's really going on.

00:39:24.312 --> 00:39:26.572
And it just gives us where
we've been able to do that

00:39:26.592 --> 00:39:30.072
successfully, it just makes us
so much smarter about serving

00:39:30.092 --> 00:39:33.292
these founders around how to guide
them on where the value really is.

00:39:33.312 --> 00:39:33.812
Yeah.

00:39:33.932 --> 00:39:35.752
I mean, I'll give you one example is

00:39:36.552 --> 00:39:38.472
because we work in these
labs, we've helped some

00:39:38.492 --> 00:39:40.852
of our companies design
their customer contracts.

00:39:41.092 --> 00:39:41.592
Hmm.

00:39:41.492 --> 00:39:43.492
Because if you have that kind
of an understanding, you can

00:39:43.512 --> 00:39:46.152
actually say, "This is really how
you should go sell as an industry.

00:39:46.432 --> 00:39:48.052
These are the key levers," and whatnot.

00:39:48.492 --> 00:39:49.812
It would take them 10 years-

00:39:49.832 --> 00:39:50.332
Yeah...

00:39:49.892 --> 00:39:51.632
to go develop that kind of an understanding.

00:39:51.652 --> 00:39:55.052
But we could just sit with them
and open up the command on that.

00:39:55.072 --> 00:39:58.192
So these become really
interesting advantages to go

00:40:01.072 --> 00:40:02.632
deal with the physics,
especially in a lot of these

00:40:02.652 --> 00:40:04.812
entrenched industries that
are trying to change with AI.

00:40:05.252 --> 00:40:08.372
And I guess the flip side of the
question is what are the challenges?

00:40:08.392 --> 00:40:11.072
Because I often think about
the very best performing

00:40:11.092 --> 00:40:15.292
venture firm is the one that
picked the right company at

00:40:15.312 --> 00:40:18.732
the right time and made that
the only bet in their portfolio.

00:40:18.912 --> 00:40:22.792
You talk about messy, kind of
walking around and not sprinting

00:40:22.812 --> 00:40:25.872
at everything and making that
one strike, and you've chosen to

00:40:25.892 --> 00:40:29.632
do a lot of things hoping, I
guess, that they all fit together.

00:40:29.652 --> 00:40:32.492
What are the challenges with that and the
trade-offs that you make in doing that?

00:40:32.512 --> 00:40:34.092
It's sort of interesting. It's actually

00:40:35.312 --> 00:40:38.912
if you look at a lot of the things
we're doing and we said, okay,

00:40:40.012 --> 00:40:41.472
so many of these things are capital

00:40:41.492 --> 00:40:44.572
entrepreneurship mindset and
the products we're creating.

00:40:44.692 --> 00:40:48.132
We're buying these living labs
or our AI transformation team.

00:40:48.372 --> 00:40:51.512
Think of those as unfair
advantages for our founders,

00:40:51.672 --> 00:40:53.952
and we're doing those to just
get smarter about what to do.

00:40:54.292 --> 00:40:58.512
But inside of investing, because I think
the last three years have been so fun.

00:40:58.652 --> 00:40:59.152
Yeah.

00:40:58.672 --> 00:41:01.672
It's just so difficult that I

00:41:02.412 --> 00:41:05.652
think we invested in the, at least for the

00:41:05.672 --> 00:41:08.032
moment, looks like it was
the right model to invest in

00:41:08.852 --> 00:41:12.752
Anthropic. We were very keen on
European sovereignty. We did Mistral.

00:41:12.772 --> 00:41:14.812
We were very keen on, hey,
defense is going to matter, so

00:41:14.832 --> 00:41:19.092
we did Anduril and Helsing in
Europe and Arafa in India.

00:41:19.712 --> 00:41:20.772
We did Mercour-

00:41:20.992 --> 00:41:21.492
Mm-hmm...

00:41:21.632 --> 00:41:23.812
at the seed. Might be the
largest shareholder there.

00:41:24.212 --> 00:41:28.912
And so it actually was reasonably
surgical, and saying who are we

00:41:28.932 --> 00:41:32.172
betting on in these different parts
as opposed to we're going to spread.

00:41:32.212 --> 00:41:32.712
Yeah.

00:41:33.412 --> 00:41:35.512
But I'll tell you though, I'm not sure from

00:41:35.532 --> 00:41:38.892
a return perspective that it's a smart
thing. Some firms index all the models,

00:41:39.992 --> 00:41:42.892
and they might actually do
better than the way we did.

00:41:42.912 --> 00:41:46.432
And we were very focused on, especially in

00:41:46.452 --> 00:41:49.732
this AI era, who has
the right set of values?

00:41:50.252 --> 00:41:51.212
Who's building? Because

00:41:52.212 --> 00:41:53.712
that's a big part of our ethos. And

00:41:55.912 --> 00:41:57.132
it wasn't even clear what these technologies

00:41:57.152 --> 00:41:59.692
were supposed to do, so we
let some time elapse too.

00:42:00.052 --> 00:42:01.012
But I think we ended up in a good spot.

00:42:01.032 --> 00:42:06.112
I think we've done really deep work
in the AI services transformation.

00:42:06.132 --> 00:42:09.052
We've built some of these industry
models like in healthcare and industrial

00:42:09.072 --> 00:42:13.132
and whatnot, and we got the right base
models and the infrastructure around it.

00:42:13.152 --> 00:42:13.652
Yeah.

00:42:13.352 --> 00:42:13.852
So

00:42:15.672 --> 00:42:17.452
I can't tell you we invested in
every single thing at the right

00:42:17.472 --> 00:42:21.292
time, but when I look at the stack
that we have, I feel like this

00:42:21.312 --> 00:42:25.092
company's going to have a key role
in diffusion of AI in the world.

00:42:26.192 --> 00:42:29.872
I guess if you look out 10 years,
20 years, your retirement day,

00:42:30.352 --> 00:42:35.272
how do you judge success at GC, and
is that exactly aligned with LPs?

00:42:35.832 --> 00:42:36.872
How do you think about that?

00:42:37.652 --> 00:42:41.532
I have a very simple metric
of success for GC that

00:42:41.552 --> 00:42:43.732
every ambitious founder says
they want to start here.

00:42:43.752 --> 00:42:44.252
Hmm.

00:42:44.620 --> 00:42:48.780
That's going back to the fact that I
think of seed, I think about creation.

00:42:48.800 --> 00:42:51.920
There's a reason why we call
our incubation fund Creation.

00:42:52.180 --> 00:42:53.560
All that is about inception.

00:42:53.700 --> 00:42:57.240
We want founders to know that they
should start with us. They can trust us.

00:42:57.920 --> 00:42:59.940
We have the scale of

00:43:00.940 --> 00:43:03.780
capabilities to help them build.
We have the environments.

00:43:03.800 --> 00:43:05.200
We have all these unfair advantages we can

00:43:05.220 --> 00:43:08.560
give them, and we are
comfortable with ambiguity.

00:43:09.180 --> 00:43:13.020
And so, if we could get there
and we're constantly trying

00:43:13.040 --> 00:43:16.920
to improve and work on that, but that's
what we obsess over. We obsess over

00:43:18.360 --> 00:43:21.620
at the beginning of a founder's
journey is where the trust builds,

00:43:21.640 --> 00:43:25.220
and the most ownership in a company
builds, and the influence builds.

00:43:25.240 --> 00:43:27.820
And if we continue to innovate

00:43:28.540 --> 00:43:30.660
in capital innovations, in go-to-market

00:43:30.680 --> 00:43:34.440
innovations, in policy innovations,
I think we can serve them

00:43:35.320 --> 00:43:37.440
the best possible. That's the aspiration.

00:43:38.200 --> 00:43:40.800
Yeah. It's almost like you're
increasing share of wallet with

00:43:40.820 --> 00:43:43.720
these really scarce assets that
are these valuable companies.

00:43:43.740 --> 00:43:48.340
Yeah, look, I think our industry
has a zero-sum game mentality, and

00:43:48.360 --> 00:43:53.500
a lot of the AI slop that you see
in social media is all directed

00:43:53.520 --> 00:43:56.900
towards, can I be top of mind for
founders when they're raising money?

00:43:57.100 --> 00:43:58.960
That's what that machine is.

00:43:59.400 --> 00:44:02.600
And what we're trying to focus
on is the founders that we

00:44:02.620 --> 00:44:06.360
work with, and I think we get
our fair share in the market.

00:44:06.380 --> 00:44:08.760
Obviously, we want to grow it, but I think

00:44:08.780 --> 00:44:11.560
we have a great set of
founders we work with.

00:44:11.580 --> 00:44:13.760
We want to take them all to Promised Land.

00:44:13.780 --> 00:44:15.860
We don't want to be the, "Well, then triage

00:44:15.880 --> 00:44:17.940
who's on the power law and focus on that."

00:44:18.280 --> 00:44:19.700
We want to make sure everybody's

00:44:20.460 --> 00:44:21.560
life's work counts.

00:44:21.580 --> 00:44:22.080
Hmm.

00:44:22.060 --> 00:44:26.000
And just because it's not on
the most obvious power law

00:44:26.020 --> 00:44:28.960
curves doesn't mean that
company couldn't be an ad-scale

00:44:28.980 --> 00:44:33.420
enduring company if you actually
helped it in certain novel ways.

00:44:33.440 --> 00:44:36.020
Since we're always looking at every
company saying, "How do we get it there?"

00:44:36.940 --> 00:44:40.700
And so that, over time, hopefully,
the reputation compounds,

00:44:40.720 --> 00:44:43.840
where founders know that no matter
what happens in terms of the

00:44:43.860 --> 00:44:46.900
journey they're going to end up being
on, because nobody knows a priori-

00:44:46.920 --> 00:44:47.420
Yeah...

00:44:47.560 --> 00:44:51.820
that this is a place where we will get
you there if there's a path to be there.

00:44:52.459 --> 00:44:53.919
Well, this was a ton of fun.

00:44:53.980 --> 00:44:55.700
I think the next few years are
going to be a lot of fun, and

00:44:55.720 --> 00:44:58.660
you're in a fascinating seat, so
thanks for the glimpse into it.

00:44:58.680 --> 00:45:00.360
Yeah, thanks for spending time with me.

00:45:00.920 --> 00:45:01.860
That was fun. That was great.

00:45:01.880 --> 00:45:02.380
Yeah, man.

00:45:02.240 --> 00:45:02.740
Thank you.

00:45:02.600 --> 00:45:03.100
Yeah.

